  "outcome": "O12",
  "verdict": {
    "outcome": "O12",
    "direction": "worsens",
    "magnitude": "moderate",
    "time_horizon": "this-parliament",
    "confidence": "low",
    "plain": "A universal tutoring guarantee for all disadvantaged pupils would require substantial, sustained public spending with no stated cost or funding source, putting pressure on the public finances. Whether long-run gains in productivity and earnings would eventually offset that cost is plausible but undemonstrated in the evidence provided.",
    "biggest_unknown": "The total fiscal cost of the guarantee is unquantified, and no independent body (OBR/IFS) has modelled whether the long-run human-capital dividend would offset it on a plausible timescale.",
    "claims": [
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "Private tutoring currently costs around \u00a327 per hour, indicating the per-pupil unit cost of any guarantee at scale would be substantial.",
        "evidence_id": "E10",
        "quote": "Private tutoring averages \u00a327 per hour"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "The predecessor National Tutoring Programme saw its subsidy progressively cut from 75% to 50% by 2023/24, with schools citing lack of funding as the main reason for not participating \u2014 illustrating the fiscal pressure even a partial subsidy creates.",
        "evidence_id": "E11",
        "quote": "these were progressively reduced from 75% to 50% by 2023/24, leading to schools citing lack of funding to top up the subsidy as a main reason for not participating or ceasing involvement."
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "The OBR would scrutinise the fiscal sustainability of a large-scale ongoing commitment of this kind, but has not issued specific analysis on this policy.",
        "evidence_id": "E12",
        "quote": "The Office for Budget Responsibility (OBR) would scrutinize the fiscal implications and sustainability of such a large-scale, ongoing commitment, though it hasn't issued specific analysis on this hypothetical policy."
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "IFS evidence shows that even large shifts in public education spending towards poorer pupils since the 1980s have not significantly reduced the attainment gap, cautioning against assuming spending alone delivers fiscal returns.",
        "evidence_id": "E25",
        "quote": "while public education spending has shifted towards poorer pupils since the 1980s, this hasn't significantly reduced the attainment gap, cautioning against assuming funding shifts alone are sufficient."
      }
    ],
    "rationale": "For O12, the core question is whether this policy improves or worsens the sustainability of public finances. The policy text commits to a universal guarantee for all disadvantaged pupils with no stated cost, funding source, or budget envelope. The NTP precedent shows that even a heavily subsidised partial programme faced mounting fiscal pressure \u2014 subsidies were cut from 75% to 50% and schools still reported funding as a barrier to participation. Scaling that up to a genuine 'guarantee' for every eligible pupil at \u00a327/hour per session would represent a large, open-ended recurrent spending commitment. No independent fiscal institution has costed this specific policy; the OBR has not modelled it. There is a theoretically plausible long-run fiscal dividend \u2014 better-educated cohorts earn more, pay more tax, and draw less on welfare \u2014 but the IFS evidence on decades of pro-poor education spending shows this chain does not reliably materialise at the macro level, and no evidence unit here quantifies a fiscal break-even point. The near-term effect on public finances is therefore a cost with no stated offset, which on O12 criteria (funded vs borrowed, debt path, consumption vs investment) scores as a worsening unless funded. The investment framing (borrowing for productive investment can improve long-run sustainability) is acknowledged, but the evidence does not support a confident projection of a net fiscal return sufficient to flip the verdict. Confidence is low because no costing exists and the magnitude depends entirely on design, take-up, and delivery model \u2014 all unspecified.",
